{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>He who is gracious to a poor man lends to YHWH, and He will repay him his reward (Proverbs 19:17).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, explained to us in this verse (Proverbs 19) the benefit of the trait of charity and the greatness of its reward in this world and in the world to come. And it is well known that the eyes of the rich man depend on his wealth or on the multitude of his friends and acquaintances, who are many, as it is written (ibid. 14): ״ואוהבי עשירי רבים״ — but the poor man's eyes depend on nothing but HaShem, blessed be He — not on his wealth, for he is poor, and not on the company of friends, for he has no friends and companions; and even if he had them, surely they would all together depart from him and hate him because of his poverty, as it is written (ibid. 19): ״כל אחי רש שנאוהו אף כי מרעהו רחקו ממנו״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ודל מרעהו יפרד״ — this means that even if only a single friend remained to him, that friend withdraws from him. Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said: since he is hated by all, the Holy One, blessed be He, loves him. And he who bestows grace upon him lends to YHWH, for nothing brought him to grace the poor man except the love of the Holy One, blessed be He — for he loves him whom the Holy One, blessed be He, loves; since the Holy One, blessed be He, loves him, calling him 'my people,' as it is said (Isaiah 14:32): ״ובה יחסו עניי עמו״. And since he lends to the Holy One, blessed be He, the Holy One, blessed be He, is obligated to repay him that loan — that is, the reward in this world; for so we find in the section on charity (Deuteronomy 14:29): ״למען יברכך ה' אלהיך״, and the blessing is addition of good and success in this world. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״מלוה ה' חונן דל״ — were it not written, it would be impossible to say it — as it were, the borrower is a servant to the lender. ״וגמולו ישלם לו״ — this is a promise regarding the reward of the world to come; for besides the Holy One, blessed be He, repaying him the loan in this world, He will give him his reward in the world to come. For it is not the way of the book of Proverbs to mention a commandment from the commandments written in the Torah unless it adds or innovates something in it, as I have mentioned many times. And as for their saying ״חונן דל״ and not 'mercy' — this is because the word חנינה is from the language of a free gift, as it is written in the section concerning the poor (Exodus 22:26): ״והיה כי יצעק אלי ושמעתי כי חנון אני״ — even though he is not worthy. Therefore man is obligated in the trait of graciousness (חנינה) because it is the trait of the Holy One, blessed be He, who bestows of His blessing upon creatures and grants them of His goodness freely; as He informed Moses at Sinai that He conducts His world with the attribute of graciousness, as it is said (Exodus 33:19): ״וחנותי את אשר אחון״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 28:9): ״והלכת בדרכיו״ — just as He gives a free gift, so you too shall give a free gift. And likewise everyone who requests from before Him and stands in prayer is answered through supplication (תחנה), which is the essence of prayer; and so we find with David, who said (Psalms 30:9): ״אליך ה' אקרא ואל ה' אתחנן״. And so we find with Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, who engaged in prayer forty days and forty nights and was not answered in all of them except through supplication (תחנה) — and this is what is written:" "<b>And I implored YHWH at that time, saying.</b> At that time — after I conquered Sihon and Og and began to wage war against the nations, I thought that perhaps the decree had been annulled. And this verse is joined to the preceding section, in which he mentioned those two kings." "<b>Saying.</b> The prayer was with incised lips, and likewise (Numbers 12:13): ״ויצעק משה אל ה' לאמר״ — its meaning is complete speech with incised lips; and I have already mentioned there the reason for this." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״ואתחנן אל ה' בעת ההיא לאמר״ — Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the universe! You called me ״עבד״ — servant, as it is said (Numbers 12:7): ״לא כן עבדי משה״. I am a servant, and Leviathan is a servant. I implore before You, and he implores before You, as it is said (Job 40:27): ״הירבה אליך תחנונים אם ידבר אליך רכות״ — you heard the supplication of Leviathan and cut a covenant with him and sustained him, as it is said (ibid.): ״היכרות ברית עמך״. And I am a servant, and You said to me (Exodus 34:10): ״הנה אנכי כורת ברית״ — but You do not uphold it; rather, You said to me (Deuteronomy 32:50): ״ומות בהר אשר אתה עולה שמה״. And not only that, but You wrote in Your Torah (Exodus 21:5): ״ואם אמר יאמר העבד אהבתי את אדוני את אשתי ואת בני לא אצא חפשי״ — I do not wish to die — ״והגישו אדוניו אל האלהים ועבדו לעולם״ — and You did not uphold this with me! I beseech You: ״שמע אלהים רנתי ואל תתעלם מתחנתי״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״רב לך״ — it is enough for you. Your adversary, your litigant, has already issued a decree against you that you shall die: Adam the first, who ate from the tree, decreed death upon all. And so Job said concerning Adam the first (Job 31:35): ״מי יתן לי שומע לי הן תוי שדי יענני וספר כתב איש ריבי״ — what book did he write? The book of the generations of Adam. Until here."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {4 "Citation 'מי יתן לי' attributed to Job 31:35; the phrase ספר כתב איש ריבי is read midrashically as referring to the book of Adam's generations (Genesis 5:1), the 'adversary' who decreed death."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:51.292378325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}